# Agree with upstream

### The command, the oracle and the fence

Week 2, Rung 1 of 7: Seed. Talk 2

---

## The command line

```ts
// variants/rung-1-seed/src/main.ts
const [courseArg, outArg] = process.argv.slice(2);
if (!courseArg) {
  process.stderr.write("usage: node src/main.ts <courseFolder> [outFile]\n");
  process.exit(2);
}
const courseFolder = resolve(courseArg);
if (!existsSync(join(courseFolder, "course.md"))) {
  process.stderr.write(`${courseFolder} has no course.md, so it is not a Tutors course.\n`);
  process.exit(1);
}
const outFile = resolve(outArg ?? join(courseFolder, "json", "tutors.json"));
const course = buildCourse(courseFolder);
mkdirSync(dirname(outFile), { recursive: true });
writeFileSync(outFile, `${JSON.stringify(course, null, 2)}\n`);
```

Exit 2 for a usage error, 1 for "not a course", 0 for done.

---

## The upstream command

```ts
// packages/jsr/tutors/main.ts (tutors-mono-repo 2b96b69)
if (!fs.existsSync("course.md")) {
  process.stdout.write("Cannot locate course.md. Please change to course folder and try again.\n");
} else {
  const srcFolder = process.cwd();
  const destFolder = `${srcFolder}/json`;
  const [course, lr] = parseCourse(srcFolder);
  generateDynamicCourse(course, destFolder);
  copyAssets(lr, destFolder);
}
```

Upstream works in the current folder and always exits 0. `tutors-cli` (`packages/jsr/tutors-cli/main.ts`)
puts it behind one program: `tutors build`, `tutors website`, `tutors validate`, ...

---

## How do we know rung 1 is right?

"Right" means: **the same `tutors.json` that upstream writes**, for every field rung 1 builds.

Not a description of upstream. Upstream itself, as an **oracle**:

```
variants/fixtures/ladder-sample-course/            the shared fixture course
variants/fixtures/ladder-sample-course.tutors.json what upstream `tutors build` wrote for it
```

Every variant, on every rung, is tested against that one file.

---

## The conformance test

```ts
// variants/rung-1-seed/tests/conformance.test.ts
const oracle = JSON.parse(readFileSync(join(FIXTURES, "ladder-sample-course.tutors.json"), "utf8"));
const course = buildCourse(join(FIXTURES, "ladder-sample-course"));

/** The fields this rung builds. Upstream has more (img, video, frontMatter, ...); RUNG.md lists them. */
const FIELDS = ["type", "id", "title", "summary", "contentMd", "route", "archiveFile", "shortTitle"];

function project(lo: Json): Json {
  const out: Json = {};
  for (const field of FIELDS) if (field in lo) out[field] = lo[field];
  if (Array.isArray(lo.los)) out.los = lo.los.map((child: Json) => project(child));
  return out;
}

test("every field this rung builds matches upstream, learning object by learning object", () => {
  assert.deepEqual(project(course), project(oracle));
});
```

---

## Two tests, two kinds of failure

```ts
test("the course tree has the same shape, ids, types and titles as upstream", () => {
  const outline = (lo: Json): unknown => [lo.type, lo.id, lo.title, ...(Array.isArray(lo.los) ? lo.los.map(outline) : [])];
  assert.deepEqual(outline(course), outline(oracle));
});
```

The outline test fails first and small when the **tree** is wrong: a missing object, a wrong order, a wrong
kind. The projection test then finds a wrong **field**. A short diff is a teaching diff.

The projection is the important design move: rung 1 claims only the fields it builds, so the test is strict
about those and silent about the rest. Adding a field means adding it to `FIELDS`.

---

## Why an oracle beats a specification

| Approach | What can go wrong |
| --- | --- |
| Read upstream, write expectations by hand | you test your reading of upstream, not upstream |
| Snapshot your own output | you freeze your own bugs |
| **Compare with upstream's output** | only a change upstream; then regenerate, and read the diff |

The upstream quirks (the lab title from `img/main.png`, one `#` dropped from a title, a unit's route
pointing at its topic) are in rung 1 **because** the oracle demanded them.

Upstream does this too: `tests/unit/course-rules/gen-conformance.test.ts` checks the validator against
what the generator leaves out.

---

## The rest of the gate

```json
"scripts": { "test": "node --test tests/*.test.ts" }
```

| File | Tests |
| --- | --- |
| `tests/course-builder.test.ts` | longest prefix, front matter, ordering, skipped folders, lab step ids, `assertNever` |
| `tests/conformance.test.ts` | agreement with the upstream oracle |
| `tests/rung-fence.test.ts` | what rung 1 may use |

`npm test` is the **Single Gate**: 11 tests, green means done. Tests write courses into a temporary folder
with `mkdtempSync`, so they need no files of their own.

---

## The Rung Fence

```ts
// variants/rung-1-seed/tests/rung-fence.test.ts
const ALLOWED = { dependencies: [], devDependencies: [], peerDependencies: [], optionalDependencies: [] };

const FORBIDDEN = [
  { pattern: /\bfetch\s*\(|XMLHttpRequest|WebSocket|"node:(https?|http2|net|tls|dgram|dns)"/,
    what: "network access", why: "... The first network call is the reader loading tutors.json in rung 5 (Reader)." },
  { pattern: /"node:child_process"|"node:worker_threads"/, what: "other processes or threads", ... },
  { pattern: /\.html\b|<html|<body/i, what: "HTML output", why: "A website arrives in rung 2 (Lite)." },
];
```

Three tests: no dependency, only `node:` and relative imports, nothing a later rung introduces.

---

## A fence failure teaches

Ask an agent on rung 1 to "also write an index page", and it adds `index.html`:

```
Rung Fence, rung 1 (Seed): src/main.ts uses HTML output (".html").
A website arrives in rung 2 (Lite). Remove it from this rung.

Lifting the fence is a person's decision: see AGENTS.md, "Ask a human first".
```

The message names the rule, the file, what was found, and the rung where it belongs. That is L4.
The agent's right move is to stop and say so, not to edit the fence.

---

## The working agreement

`variants/rung-1-seed/AGENTS.md` is a Short Manifest (L2), under 500 words:

- **Verify**: `npm test`. If the fence or the conformance test fails, change the code, not the test
- **Where things go**: a new kind goes in `src/model.ts`, "then the `case` the compiler asks for"
- **Rung Fence**: what the fence checks, in three lines
- **Ask a human first**: any dependency, the fence, the fixture and its oracle, the conformance test,
  `AGENTS.md` itself, the shape of `tutors.json`

Only what a test cannot check stays in the prose.

---

## Summary

- `main.ts` is the only file that knows about `process`; `buildCourse` is a plain function
- the conformance test makes upstream the judge, field by field
- the fence keeps rung 1 on Node alone, and says which rung brings what it refuses
- `npm test` is the one gate

Read the note "Making mistakes unrepresentable", then do Lab 2.
